  "enhanced_information": "The 4-year-old boy presents with periorbital and lower extremity edema, significant proteinuria (4+), and low serum albumin (2.6 g/dL), indicating nephrotic syndrome. The normal serum complement levels suggest minimal change disease, which is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. Prednisone is the first-line treatment for minimal change disease, aligning with the consensus in pediatric nephrology. The reasoning chains correctly identify the nephrotic syndrome and the appropriate management with prednisone, while the retrieved evidence paths support the relationship between edema, proteinuria, and nephrotic syndrome. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is prednisone therapy.",
  "analysis": "The clinical presentation of the 4-year-old boy, including periorbital and lower extremity edema, significant proteinuria (4+), and low serum albumin (2.6 g/dL), is indicative of nephrotic syndrome. The normal serum complement levels further support the diagnosis of minimal change disease, which is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. In such cases, the first-line treatment is prednisone therapy, as it has been shown to be effective in inducing remission. Other options such as enalapril and furosemide may be considered for managing hypertension or edema but are not the primary treatment for the underlying condition. Anti-streptolysin O levels are not relevant in this context, as the boy's symptoms do not suggest a post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, and cyclosporine is typically reserved for steroid-resistant cases. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is to initiate prednisone therapy.",
